"""Replace observational notes with a continuous defense talk, preserving citations."""
from pathlib import Path
import re

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
PATH = ROOT / 'thesis_docs/PRESENTATION_SPEAKER_SCRIPT.md'
SOURCE = PATH.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
HEADINGS = list(re.finditer(r'^## Slide (\d+): (.+)$', SOURCE, re.M))
assert len(HEADINGS) == 43 and [int(m[1]) for m in HEADINGS] == list(range(1, 44))

SPOKEN = {
1: """Good day. Our study asks a practical question: can a chat moderation system recognize a concerning conversation by following how it develops, rather than judging each message in isolation? GroomShield combines language context with a model of the conversation sequence. We built the module, compared it with simpler methods on the same conversations, and measured what happens when we prioritize finding positives. I will show the design first, then the evidence behind that choice.""",
2: """I am presenting the work of Andrei Torres, Don Idos, Justin Arroco, and Michael Maala, advised by Manuel Calimlim Jr. We approached this as both a modeling problem and a moderation problem: a useful result must identify more conversations that deserve attention while making the review cost visible. That is the question that organizes the presentation.""",
3: """In chat, a single line often tells us too little. An ordinary-sounding message can mean something different after several exchanges, and a risk signal can appear gradually rather than all at once. A moderator who sees the conversation has context that a word filter does not. We designed GroomShield to capture some of that accumulating context and produce a conversation-level priority for review.""",
4: """Existing tools still have a role. A keyword filter can catch explicit terms quickly, and user reports can bring cases to a moderator. Their weakness is the period before an obvious word appears or a person decides to report. During that period, meaning may be carried by repeated requests, changes in tone, or the way two speakers interact. Our study tests whether following those changes improves detection over static methods.""",
5: """The problem we set out to solve was therefore broader than spotting a suspicious sentence. We wanted a module that could interpret recent wording, retain evidence across turns, and decide which complete conversations should receive human attention. We developed that architecture first. We then selected PAN-2012 because its ordered conversations and persistent speaker identifiers let us evaluate this design under a controlled partition.""",
6: """The research questions lead from the existing practice to our proposed module. We ask what keyword and rule-based approaches can detect, where they lose conversational meaning, and how machine learning can follow behavior across a chat. The final question is whether that complete module finds more positive conversations and reduces misses. Those questions determine the comparisons we report; they are not separate promises to label every message or predict the exact start of grooming.""",
7: """The approved objectives follow the same logic. First, assess the limits of conventional approaches. Second, build a module that combines conversational context with behavioral sequence analysis. Third, test whether that module reduces missed positive conversations. The general objective is an AI moderation module that strengthens human review. We kept these objectives intact and use the next alignment tables to show the method and evidence for each one.""",
8: """For the first objective, we needed concrete comparators. We implemented a keyword rule and a method that takes the highest contextual score seen in a conversation. Both were tested on the same held-out conversations as the sequence models. The keyword rule found 27 of the 44 positives and missed 17. That result gives us a measured baseline for the question about static moderation. We did not run a separate field trial of an operational user-report system.""",
9: """The second objective is demonstrated by the working two-layer module and review interface. The third is answered by the controlled result: under our recall-oriented F2 policy, the Trajectory-Feature LSTM found 41 of 44 positive conversations and missed three, compared with six misses for the weighted scorer. The other LSTM variant found all 44, with a higher false-alert cost. We will return to that trade-off in the results section, because improving coverage alone is not the entire moderation decision.""",
10: """Here is what we actually built. DistilBERT processes a turn with its recent context. From those scores and the interaction, we derive seven changing signals across the conversation. An LSTM learns from their order and produces a conversation score. We also tested a second LSTM that receives text embeddings along with the seven features. The output helps a moderator decide what to inspect; the model does not itself make a sanction decision.""",
11: """We selected PAN-2012 for the evaluation because it has ordered chats and speaker identifiers, which this sequence design requires. Our measured positive endpoint is a conversation containing an author on the official predator list. Layer 1 learns an author-derived proxy, and Layer 2 uses the unfolding signals to classify the conversation. This matters when interpreting the results: the final counts describe conversations, while turn scores are model inputs rather than independently verified grooming labels for individual messages.""",
12: """The study's contribution is the combination of a working architecture and a controlled comparison. We can ask whether sequence modeling improves on static use of the same features, not merely whether the prototype produces a score. The two kinds of error also have real consequences for a review workflow: a miss leaves a positive conversation unflagged, while a false alert consumes moderator attention. That balance will guide how we interpret the results.""",
13: """Our design has two intellectual foundations. Language models help interpret a message in its immediate context; sequence models can retain how the evidence changes as the conversation continues. The literature also treats grooming-related interaction as a developing process rather than a fixed phrase. We use those ideas to motivate the architecture, then test the architecture against simpler alternatives.""",
14: """Context and sequence answer different questions. DistilBERT helps with what a turn means in relation to nearby turns. The LSTM addresses what has been happening across the conversation: whether signals persist, rise, retreat, or change in combination. A single high score could be accidental or isolated. A sequence model can learn how such scores behave over time, which is the specific advantage we set out to evaluate.""",
15: """The Online Grooming Discourse Model, or OGDM, motivated our view of interaction as something that can develop, overlap, and recur. It did not give us ready-made labels for each stage of a chat. Instead, we translated that theory into measurable changes that a sequence model could learn from. After defining that method, we chose PAN-2012 for its chronology and speaker IDs, not because the research questions were built around that dataset.""",
16: """The table shows that translation. Current and peak scores let the model see whether contextual evidence appears and persists. A rising score or a spike followed by a drop captures change rather than a fixed keyword. Topic distance and turn-taking add information about where the exchange moves and how the two speakers participate. We call these theory-informed indicators, not direct OGDM stage labels. Their purpose is to give the LSTM a structured history to learn from.""",
17: """The methodology had to protect the result from an easy source of leakage: the same person appearing in both training and test. I will explain the processing path, the author-disjoint partition, and the matched comparison. Then I will show how validation sets the conversation cutoff. Keeping these decisions separate lets us say what the trained models learned and what changed when we later compared operating policies.""",
18: """A new turn enters the system with up to two previous turns for contextual scoring. Layer 1 produces an author-derived proxy score; a fixed encoder also supplies a current-turn embedding. We form seven behavioral features from the evolving conversation. One LSTM receives those seven features, while the other receives the features plus 768 embedding values. Both follow the sequence and return a conversation score. A validation-selected cutoff converts that score into a review flag, leaving the final judgment to a moderator.""",
19: """Before training, we preserve turn order and conversation identity and keep only valid two-speaker chats for this experiment. We build the keyword list and benign reference from training data so test conversations cannot shape those resources. Validation then chooses checkpoints and the operating cutoffs. The held-out scores remain fixed for our later F0.5, F1, and F2 comparison. That separation is the basis for interpreting the results fairly.""",
20: """Author separation is stronger than simply splitting rows. Suppose one conversation has authors A and B and another has B and C. B connects those conversations, so the whole connected group belongs in one partition. We apply that rule across the dataset and verify that authors do not overlap between training, validation, and test. It prevents a model from benefiting from a familiar participant's language when we claim to evaluate on unseen author groups.""",
21: """Our key comparison holds the input features constant. The weighted scorer combines the seven trajectory features without learning their order. The Trajectory-Feature LSTM receives the same seven features but learns from their sequence. If it improves, that is evidence for the value of the selected sequence aggregator over this static combination. The Embedding-and-Feature LSTM receives additional text information, so we report it alongside the others without pretending it is the same matched-input experiment.""",
22: """Positive conversations are uncommon in the test set, so one percentage cannot tell the full story. Recall tells us how many of the 44 positives we found. Precision tells us how many alerts were actually positive, and the false-positive count shows how much review work those alerts create. PR-AUC describes ranking across cutoffs. We use F2 for the main operating comparison because it gives more weight to recall while still counting precision. We also show the actual missed and false-alert counts.""",
23: """The cutoff policy deserves its own explanation. F0.5 favors precision, F1 balances precision and recall, and F2 favors recall. Our research emphasis is reducing missed positive conversations, so F2 is the main reporting policy. We choose each cutoff on validation, not on the test set. The original evaluation used F0.5; afterward, we compared validation-selected F1 and F2 cutoffs on the preserved test scores. The model weights did not change. The three-policy tables will show exactly what that choice costs and gains.""",
24: """The results now answer two connected questions. First, under the recall-oriented F2 policy, do the sequence models improve on the simpler methods? Second, how do detection and false alerts change if we choose F0.5 or F1 instead? Once we understand those outcomes, we can judge whether the local prototype processes a turn quickly enough to justify further deployment testing.""",
25: """We trained on 13,031 conversations, used 1,827 for validation, and held out 1,862 for test. Only 44 test conversations were positive, so every additional miss changes recall meaningfully. The partitions have zero author overlap under the connected-group rule. The original F0.5 evaluation and the later F1 and F2 comparisons use this same test partition; the later comparisons reuse saved scores with cutoffs chosen on validation. All counts that follow refer to conversations.""",
26: """This table gives the main F2 result. The Trajectory-Feature LSTM found 41 of 44 positives and missed three, while producing 21 false alerts. The Embedding-and-Feature LSTM found all 44, with 41 false alerts. Both exceed the weighted scorer's 38 detections, although the two LSTMs make a different coverage-versus-workload trade-off. For the matched seven-feature comparison, the trajectory model also has stronger PR-AUC and F2 than the weighted scorer; the paired analysis behind that comparison appears shortly.""",
27: """The bars separate two aspects of performance. PR-AUC asks whether the model tends to rank positive conversations above negatives across cutoffs. F2 describes the chosen recall-oriented operating point. The embedding model has the highest PR-AUC point estimate, while the trajectory model has the highest F2 point estimate. These observations do not establish one LSTM as universally better. The more important comparison for the sequence claim remains the trajectory model versus weighted scoring on the same seven inputs.""",
28: """Coverage is easiest to understand as people reviewing conversations: how many of the 44 positives would enter the review queue? The keyword rule sends 27, weighted scoring 38, the trajectory model 41, and the embedding model all 44. Maximum Layer 1 sends 39. The LSTMs therefore cover more of the positive set under F2. But a review queue also contains mistakes, so coverage has to be read together with the false-alert counts on the next slide.""",
29: """Here the cost of greater coverage becomes concrete. The trajectory model cuts weighted scoring's six misses to three and also lowers false alerts from 24 to 21. The embedding model has no misses on these 44 positives but creates 41 false alerts. Maximum Layer 1 catches 39 positives yet produces 78 false alerts. Those are conversation counts, not individual messages. The question for an actual review team is how many extra alerts are acceptable for each additional positive found, which is why we tested other cutoff policies.""",
30: """With the embedding model's weights held fixed, changing the cutoff changes that balance sharply. F0.5 finds 41 positives with six false alerts. F1 finds 43 with 14. F2 finds all 44 but produces 41 false alerts. Going from F1 to F2 therefore catches one more positive at the cost of 27 more false alerts in this test. We retain F2 as our recall-first reporting policy, while F1 is a serious candidate for a review workflow with limited capacity. That choice needs independent workflow evaluation.""",
31: """The same middle-ground story does not hold for every model. With the trajectory model, F0.5 and F1 both find 40 positives, while F1 adds two false alerts. F2 finds one additional positive and raises false alerts to 21. So we should not describe F1 as automatically optimal simply because it sits between F0.5 and F2. The tables make the decision transparent, and the model comparison itself must still be supported statistically.""",
32: """For the matched seven-feature comparison, we resampled whole author-connected groups 2,000 times and compared the trajectory LSTM with weighted scoring each time. The estimated gain is 0.1103 in PR-AUC and 0.0630 in F2, with both paired 95 percent intervals above zero. That supports the claim that the selected sequence model improves on static scoring of the same features. The observed miss reduction is real in this test, although its separate recall-difference interval includes zero. This is the evidence behind our sequence-model conclusion.""",
33: """The remaining trajectory-model misses help identify what to study next. Those positive conversations had only 2, 11, and 26 turns. Each is shorter than the 36-turn lower quartile of the positives that model detected. This suggests short-history performance deserves targeted testing. It does not identify the exact reason for each miss, and we did not have independently annotated grooming-onset times. The next study should assess fixed conversation prefixes before making an early-detection claim.""",
34: """We also tested the complete local scoring path, because a turn-by-turn prototype has to respond within a usable time. Across 160 sequential requests per model on this CPU, the trajectory model's median was 267 milliseconds and the embedding model's was 246. Their p95 times were about 709 and 718 milliseconds, while the embedding model had a longer p99 tail. The demo recomputes the full history after every turn, so longer conversations take longer. These timings establish local feasibility for the tested prefixes; concurrent users and production hardware remain a separate evaluation.""",
35: """We can now answer the objectives directly. The static methods exposed measurable coverage and false-alert limits. We delivered the contextual and sequence-based module with a working review interface. Under F2, the trajectory model reduced weighted scoring's six misses to three, and the embedding model found all 44 positives with a larger alert workload. The matched-input paired result supports the benefit of sequence modeling. Together, these results advance the general objective of strengthening conversation-level review while making the cutoff trade-off explicit.""",
36: """The next step is to test the fixed models and cutoffs on contemporary chats and observe how moderators handle the alerts. In particular, the embedding model's F1 setting found 43 positives with 14 false alerts, compared with 44 and 41 under F2. That is a meaningful workload choice, and an independent study should measure both missed-case costs and review capacity before choosing an operating point. We also need concurrent-load testing and a separate expert-annotated study before assessing grooming onset.""",
37: """GroomShield shows that following a conversation's development can strengthen chat review. The trajectory model has a supported advantage over static scoring of the same inputs, and the embedding model offers the highest observed coverage under F2. The alternative cutoffs show the practical price of that coverage rather than hiding it. Our contribution is a working two-layer system and a clear evidence base for deciding how to use it in a human review workflow. Thank you. We welcome your questions.""",
38: """If asked about the baselines under other cutoffs, this backup table contains the complete counts. Maximum Layer 1 rises from 23 to 39 detected positives as we move from F0.5 to F2, while false alerts rise from 18 to 78. Weighted scoring makes identical F0.5 and F1 decisions, then finds two more positives under F2 with 11 more false alerts. The keyword rule is binary, so its decisions do not change across the three policies. Every row uses the same 1,862 test conversations.""",
}

assert set(SPOKEN) == set(range(1, 39))
parts = []
approved_objectives = None
for i, match in enumerate(HEADINGS):
    number = int(match[1])
    end = HEADINGS[i + 1].start() if i + 1 < len(HEADINGS) else len(SOURCE)
    original = SOURCE[match.end():end]
    citation = re.search(r'^(?:Source|Sources): .+$', original, re.M)
    assert citation, f'No citation for slide {number}'
    if number == 7:
        ref = re.search(r'^Exact approved wording:\s*(.+)$', original, re.M | re.S)
        assert ref
        approved_objectives = ref[1].strip()
    spoken = SPOKEN.get(number)
    if spoken is None:
        spoken = original[:citation.start()].strip()
    parts.append(f'## Slide {number}: {match[2]}\n\n{spoken}\n\n{citation[0]}')

assert approved_objectives and '__General Objective:__' in approved_objectives
header = ("# Thesis Defense Speaker Script\n\n"
          "Updated September 30, 2026. Read-aloud notes match the 43-slide "
          "**WASD - Thesis 2 - F2 and latency revised.pptx**. Slides 1-37 are the main talk. "
          "Slide 38 is a hidden evidence backup; slides 39-43 are retained hidden template resources. "
          "Citations and the approved objective wording below are presenter references, not lines to read aloud.\n\n")
new = header + '\n\n'.join(parts) + '\n\n## Presenter reference: exact approved objectives\n\n' + approved_objectives + '\n'
PATH.write_text(new, encoding='utf-8')
print(f'Rewrote {len(SPOKEN)} spoken notes; preserved 43 source citations and exact approved objectives as reference.')
